Current debate, focused primarily on feminist issues, silences an alternative reality: men's distress in the face of their own fears and moral or physical obligations. Even if society is gradually relieving men of some responsibilities, men have nonetheless inherited their own mental load. Expectations are immense and, for some, seem insurmountable. Men are now expected to satisfy women sexually, while also facing the added complexity of having to familiarize themselves with the range of women's desires, freed from inhibition by the liberation of speech. We are bombarded with tutorials and books purporting to explore every female fantasy and the ramifications of women's desires. Despite the abundance of resources offered, the subject has never seemed so obscure. Men must navigate this minefield, weighed down by daunting claims such as: the female orgasm is much more powerful than the male orgasm, most women fake it, the female orgasm is very difficult to achieve... Comedian Blanche Gardin describes this phenomenon in her sketch "Being a Woman, Being a Man": "the first challenge a man faces in life is having to get an erection [...] it is a monstrous form of servitude."

In social terms, it is unthinkable not to work or not to provide for the household...
In romantic life, approaching someone is risky. Following the example of German and Scandinavian men, many have somewhat given up and let women take the lead, thereby ensuring consent. Several admit to watching their words very carefully "to avoid being accused of bad faith" or complicity with rape culture. Others have even changed their behavior.

In the workplace, making sexist jokes has become complicated. The obligation of gender parity has created mental exhaustion because it requires constant vigilance. First identified in the United States in 2009, this phenomenon is called "[https://www.eveprogramme.com/33358/gender-fatigue-concept/#:~:text=Du%20%C2%AB%20dilemme%20id%C3%A9ologique%20%C2%BB%20%C3%A0%20la,diversit%C3%A9%20et%20du%20management%20interg%C3%A9n%C3%A9rationnel. gender fatigue]."<br>
According to a survey conducted for Le Parisien by OpinionWay (in June 2018, with a sample of 1055 men), the fallout from the Weinstein affair was perceived as:
"tiring" by 47%;
"excessive" by 45%;
"annoying" by 42%;
and 38% of working men said they had had to change their behavior and/or question themselves.
